Date: Thu,  6 Jan 94 21:15:37 PST
From: Blanc Weber <blancw@microsoft.com>
Subject: SOAKING TAXPAYERS

There have been several agreeable posts about the above;  I think 
everyone deserves
a point, but just one more thing: I must clarify my position in reply 
to Sandy's post,
concerning the issue of whether participation in accepting government 
dole is/is not theft:

	Well yes, but what is your point?  There are all kinds of
	"accessories," but the issue is theft.  Does being an "accessory
	after the fact" *necessarily* constitute theft?  If the answer is
	"no," then what point is there in making global judgments about
	accepting government funds?
......................
Well, if there is no point in making global judgements about it,
then I wonder why we don't all just accept our fate in peace
and quiet and not get so emotional about the whole taxing
business.   I think it's because we need to keep straight accounts
with our own philosophy/morality, not only in private, but in public
as well.

I was pointing to the fact that the image of complicity is
encouraging to goverment types, rather than discouraging,
as I would expect Extropians would want to be, and that the act doesn't
really get one anywhere near to the ideal of achieving individual
sovereignty, as I expect anarchists to desire to have.

There is the public image and the private image of being
either agreeable or disagreeable to government funding
supported by taxation.  Someone may have their own hidden
agenda for accepting what is offered, but in the government's
eyes it looks like agreeableness to their programs.   Since governments
find all sorts of excuses for what they do, anytime that anyone
participates in any way with what they offer, this presents to them
the equivalence of moral acceptance of the whole program -
even if it really isn't, even the recipient is only really trying to "undo
what has been done".

Underneath it all there may be all sorts of games being played by
everyone who is sharing the same cage, but in appearance it still
looks like the same old status quo, regardless of all the fine
reasons that there are for playing inside.
("From the movie A Streetcar Named Desire:  "We don't live together,
we just share the same cage.")

I'm not advocating that people should refrain from make their own,
imaginative decisions about how they will deal with the fact that we are,
presently, without choice in having our money taken away from us and
with but few avenues for justice. "Welfare mothers" aren't thieves,
they just look like static members of society.

Blanc





