Date: Thu, 6 Jan 1994 21:55:45 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Reputation

I've changed the name of this thread to a simple "Reputation," as it
is a truly core subject, one we often allude to (as in
"reputation-based systems") but which remains elusive.

I agree completely with Kathy Camus' (or Cadmus's, as you will)
analysis: reputations are an emergent thing which others have
effectively "voted" on.

A politician who has won an election may be said to have a higher
reputation in some sense than his opponents. But only because enough
people voted for him, in the same way that Ford now has an improved
reputation compared to earlier years because more people are buying
(voting for) Ford cars and trucks. But a politician does not "own"
his reputation, he cannot "demand" to be relected, just as Ford cannot
claim to own their reputation.

How reputation-based systems behave is worthy of much more discussion
here. For example, to use Craig Presson's example from his message on
this, suppose Alice starts claiming that Bob's products are defective,
crummy, whatever. Whether she claims this is her opinion or based on
scientific analysis is irrelevant: she can affect Bob's reputation
only ot the extent that she herself has a reputation.

Craig's example of "personal opinion" being different from claims of
defects is confused. Suppose I announce that the film "Schindler
Missed" is a crummy film...my personal opinion. No damage done, as
nobody cares what I think. So far, so good. How about Roger Ebert
claiming "Schindler Missed" is a crummy film? Major damage, because of
Ebert's fame and reputation as a film critic. Many people trust Siskel
and Ebert, and either of them can greatly affect a film's success.

But can we claim that Ebert has "stolen" or "damaged" the property of
another (presumably the owners of the film, which may or may not be
the director, actors, distributors, etc.)? Of course not. That way
lies madness.

And if Roger Ebert can help a film's reputation, can he not go to the
film's "reputation owner" and demand a share of the increased profits?
Isn't that the flip side of collecting damages from those who "harm"
reputations? A pretty crazy world if this was so, eh?

What happens is that Roger Ebert "spends some of his reputation
capital" by giving postivie or negative reviews. That is, others
increase or decrease their ratings of him (how much they listen to his
advice in the future, etc.) based on their continuing assessment of
his views. 

(The phrase "spending reputation capital" is a kind of term of art in
the Bay Area agorics/libertarian/Cypherpunks community...I have no
idea if the economics literature uses it of if there's a more formal
definition that is more commonly used.)

Thus, the person who screams "Wolf!" of "This car will kill you!" too
often will end up being ignored, or may even have _negative_
reputation capital. Net.loons like L.Detweiler are a good example of
this....am I to sue him because he calls me the Antichrist? Of course
not. Nor could I sue, as I see things, a respected member of the Net
like Mike Godwin or Gene Spafford if they began insulting me or
calling me incompetent. 

Sure, others _might_ have their opinions of me changed, but so what? I
don't _own_ their opinions of me, which in the aggregate is all a
reputation really is: a complex set of opinions of other players in
the game, other agents.

And if I am really _not_ the fool that Godwin and Spafford claim, then
over time I have general faith that _their_ reputations will suffer to
some extent for having misjudged me. That's the way things really do
work, eventually, in so many areas of life.

To expect a court system or any other legal system to deal with these
sujective issues is unrealistic, and dangerous to boot.

And you can't really have anything resembling free speech once you're on
the slippery slope of suing for damages. Check out the British system,
where newspapers are unable to print lots of things because of an even
more liberal interpretation of the idea of "damages" than we have
here.

I say let people say anything they wish, write anything they wish,
etc. But teach kids not to believe everything they hear. Rating
agencies, independent testing labs, and credentialling services in a
free market will do the rest.

I'll close with a hypothetical exchange between me, the believer in
reputations as emergent properties, and "Other," a person who believes
his reputation is owned by himself and no other:

Me: "You say Bob's engines fall apart after the warranty is up? Show me
some proof. Meanwhile, BlackNet Ratings reports an average lifetime of
8.31 years, 5 years past the warranty. I trust them more than I trust you."

Other: "You don't trust me? Sir, you have defamed my reputation and I
shall thus see you in court."

Me: "Fuck you."

Other: "!!!!!!!"


--Tim May, whose "reputation" on the Hawthorne Exchange is determined
by a combination of the ongoing value of "KLAUS" and other shares he
controls (ANARCHY, GUNS, PGP,...), and by the total valuation of his
account (which in turn depended on his past valuation, his astuteness
in trading reputations, etc.)

If someone sells my shares, can I sue them for "damaging" my reputation?


