Date: Thu, 6 Jan 1994 23:31:18 -0800
From: moulton@netcom.com (Fred C. Moulton)
Subject: Libel (Was: No More Prisons)

From: Craig Presson <fcp@moontarz.nuance.com>

>When you sell a business, you place a value on assets, liabilities, 
>future earnings, and goodwill. The market treats goodwill as a good
>and pays money for it; thus it is much more "property-like" than you
>are admitting.

Let us be very careful here.  While it is correct that "goodwill" is
given a value when a business is sold, this does not indicate that
the goodwill is an item which could be sold independently.  For example
many years ago Memorex decided to get out of the business of selling
audio tape, Memorex sold the buildings, machines, processes, etc to 
Tandy Corp and I have been told by someone who worked there at the time
that the documents were drawn up to show that the rights Tandy was 
purchasing to use the Memorex name were worth more than the buildings,
machines, processes, etc.  Memorex showed the value it placed on the
use of the Memorex name. 

Another example is the company I work for, Sun Microsystems, which has
some goodwill in the computer marketplace and in valuing the company
there would probably be an entry for goodwill.  However Scott (the boss)
can not turn to one of the VPs and say "Sell of 15% of our goodwill so
that we can use the money to fund research on immanentizing the Eschaton".
Sun's goodwill is closely tied to its name, logos, etc.

One way that a company can sell gain money from divesting goodwill is
when there are brands or divisions which have identities which are
valuable.  Consider Arm & Hammer Baking Soda, it is made by the Arm & 
Hammer division of Church and Dwight Co. Inc.  Now consider if I had
plants which produced backing soda and sold it as Moulton's Backing
Soda.  Now I buy the exclusive rights to the Arm & Hammer name, box
design, etc from Church & Dwight and now I sell my baking soda as
Arm & Hammer Baking Soda and the fine print on the box says Arm & Hammer
is a product of Moulton Enterprises, Inc.  Since I own exclusive
rights, Church and Dwight now sells their baking soda as Church and
Dwight's Baking Soda.  Guess which sells in higher volume? 

My opinion is that when considering goodwill when valuing a company,
a better discription to use is "market value of brand name, logo, etc"
since this better describes what you are buying.  The market value
reflects the reputation of the brand among consumers and the value of 
the brand is selling products.

Fred

