That was what left me unconvinced in Kathy's message. Obviously the
"reputation == property" analogy is only partial, as you can't sell
your reputation as you can your boat (even if you sell it on HEx, you
don't thereby lose it -- HEx separates the _value_ of the reputation
from the Ding an Sich, already a misnomer because reputation is
relational and abstract as opposed to Thingish), yet a reputation is
property-like in the sense that you gain it through labor and trade,
and in the case of a business, get paid for it when you sell the whole
thing.

The fact that the _value_ of your reputation is set on the market
only likens it to most marketable assets, most notably stocks. 

I don't see what sense of "own" implies "demand" in the sentence
above. The politician and the automaking corporation can't directly
_control_ the value of their reputation (as I sit and will my stocks
to go up by 50% at opening tomorrow, to no avail), and yet this very
reputation is used by them to sell cars and get votes. Doing this is a
form of "demanding" a reward but the reputation is not automatically
diminished by doing so -- so it's like an asset that you can trade
repeatedly as long as you don't abuse it. Hmmmmmmm.

> Craig's example of "personal opinion" being different from claims of
> defects is confused.
Gee, I thot I explained that pretty well, too. Shux. Again:

Under current law, to prove libel you have to show _falsehood_ and
_credibility_. Truth is a perfect defense against a libel suit; less
perfect, but still effective, is a showing that no reasonable person
would have believed what was said. The essence of the suit is that a
knowing falsehood was written or spoken with intent to harm a person's
reputation and therefore livelihood. Your arguments for "let the
reader beware" are beguiling but ignore the fraudulent nature of a
true act of libel. So it may matter whether Alice (what's a
cryptographer doing in this discussion?) says "I think ..." or "Tests
prove ...", since falsehood is testable and credibility higher in the
second case.

