From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: trans- and post-

Steve Witham: 
> I think even uploads will be mostly human, and that's the
> distinguishing thing for me.

This is an interesting comment.  For a very brief period I think
you will be right: a paranoid new uploader will want to assure
that "all of me is here" via rigorous authentication of emotions, senses, 
even thinking speed.  But soon many uploads will recognize that
human vestiges no longer serve useful functions as uploads,
and will shrug them off in favor of emotions and habits of thought
that lead to success in a computer world.  Even before uploading we 
are starting to alter our strongest instincts with sleeping and diet 
pills, anti-depressants, euphorics, hallucinogens, etc. because
modern society is so different from hunter-gatherer tribes that
formed our instincts.  The change to uploader society will be even
more radical, requiring even more rapid self-transformation to not
get left behind in the bit-bucket of history.

> This is *related* to the idea of the Singularity in the 
> sense that posthuman and postsingularity are both defined
> in terms of unrecognizeability.  

Some basic aspects of uploader society are predictable
into as far in the future as you care to name; I don't foresee
any era that is completely unrecognizable now.  If on the other
hand there will be such an era, it wouldn't be useful to speculate 
about it in any way, or give it a label, or worry about it in any
other fashion, for then we are attempting to give form to
something that by definition must be completely formless in
our current primitive minds.

> if, say, things followed that kind of curve over a few
> orders of magnitude of difference.  It would be a 
> singularity in the same sense that a population explosion

Most of the things our society considers most important would be
recognizable to, for example, folks from pre-explosion Elizabethan 
England.

