Date: Fri, 7 Jan 1994 01:15:36 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: Intermittent supplement use?

Carl writes:
>Cross-examination revealed that some small percentage of the population are
>`gaggers': we have esophagi that sieze up when you touch certain areas of
>the back of the mouth.  Sounds like you're one of us!  Short of local
>anaesthesia, there's not a damn thing anyone can do about it.  Gagging is
>like sneezing for a hay fever sufferer: try as you may, you just can't help
>it.
>
I had this odd thought:  would a squirt of sore-throat spray, like
Chloraseptic, help anesthetize your throat enough to counteract the gag
reflex?  This might allow you to take some medications that don't come in
liquid form.

There's also the idea of pouring powder out of capsules and putting it in
liquid, or of grinding up tablets with a mortar and pestle and putting
those in liquid (or sprinkling them on the peanut butter in a sandwich, or
whatever).
If you sometimes gag, but not always, maybe a suggestion I saw in a book
will help:  take a drink before taking the tablet to help lubricate your
throat, and use a lot of water with it.  Maybe practicing swallowing large
mouthfuls of water without pills in them will help, and maybe not.  Making
sure to get coated pills might help, too -- they slip down easier.  Maybe
if you can't get coated ones, putting a little vegetable oil or something
on them might help.

I used to have this problem, but I don't much any more.  I started by
taking little pills with LOTS of water (enough that I'd have to look in my
hand and the cup to make sure I'd taken the pill and not dropped it), then
bigger pills with lots of water, then little pills with less water, and so
on.  After years of practice, I can now take little pills with no water and
three horse pills with one big gulp (I couldn't do this six months ago, so
progress is clearly possible).  Not like it makes me more virtuous or
anything, but it _is_ more convenient.

Just my random ideas.  I hope they are of some use.

