>rate of change of the rate of change, etc.
>
>"Singularity" is a term of art in mathematical physics, and denotes a place
>where a differential-equation-based physical theory predicts infinite rates
>of change, and thus breaks down.  For example, the current theory of
>gravity predicts infinite accelerations at the center of a black hole. 
>This indicates that we need a better theory of gravity.

So the conclusion must be that we need a better theory of expropy. What
formal investigestions of the rate of change (of most things) have been
made? If we look at some special technology we often see an (approx)
exponential growth, followed by rapid decay when new technology takes over.
However, the overall measure looks like growing exponentially.

This is an interesting topic, but most of what I've seen so far seem more
like speculations (but what is science but speculations).

